{"name":"Port Glow","hex":"#54383b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"port","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place on the coast at which ships can shelter, or dock to load and unload cargo or passengers.","A town or city containing such a place, a port city."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to port, the left-hand side of a vessel when facing the bow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/port"},{"term":"glow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To emit heat and light without a flame.","To emit heat and light without a flame."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of heat and light being emitted by a hot object.","A state of heat being emitted by a person or an animal's body."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Port","blocks":[{"text":"A port is a maritime facility comprising one or more wharves or loading areas, where ships load and discharge cargo and passengers. Although usually situated on a sea coast or estuary, ports can also be found far inland, such as Hamburg, Manchester and Duluth; these access the sea via rivers or canals. Because of their roles as ports of entry for immigrants as well as soldiers in wartime, many port cities have experienced dramatic multi-ethnic and multicultural changes throughout their histories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/330px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/La_Lanterna_di_Genova_di_notte.jpg/500px-La_Lanterna_di_Genova_di_notte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/La_Lanterna_di_Genova_di_notte.jpg/1280px-La_Lanterna_di_Genova_di_notte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars